WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.720 --> 00:00:03.360
MARTIN: Today we will talk
about a large enterprise

00:00:03.360 --> 00:00:05.610
called Big Corp, Limited.

00:00:05.610 --> 00:00:09.090
They want to centralize access
control to their web apps,

00:00:09.090 --> 00:00:11.770
both internal and external.

00:00:11.770 --> 00:00:15.203
Charlie, how could they do that
with Google Cloud platform?

00:00:15.203 --> 00:00:16.620
CHARLIE: Well,
right now there are

00:00:16.620 --> 00:00:18.703
a lot of different groups
of admins and developers

00:00:18.703 --> 00:00:20.460
controlling websites
at Big Corp, Limited,

00:00:20.460 --> 00:00:23.340
and they use a lot of different
frameworks and languages.

00:00:23.340 --> 00:00:25.770
We're going to see how Google
Cloud platform can help them

00:00:25.770 --> 00:00:27.900
control access to
all those sites

00:00:27.900 --> 00:00:29.250
without having to change them.

00:00:29.250 --> 00:00:31.360
It will all be centralized
control instead.

00:00:31.360 --> 00:00:34.825
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:41.760 --> 00:00:43.650
MARTIN: Let me introduce
you to Marissa.

00:00:43.650 --> 00:00:46.650
She's a systems administrator
at Big Corp, Limited,

00:00:46.650 --> 00:00:48.690
and her team has
to manage access

00:00:48.690 --> 00:00:50.830
to all these
different web systems.

00:00:50.830 --> 00:00:52.710
She has to solve all
of these problems

00:00:52.710 --> 00:00:55.350
that Charlie told us
about a minute ago.

00:00:55.350 --> 00:00:58.140
Marissa and her team has
outlined four different use

00:00:58.140 --> 00:00:59.380
cases.

00:00:59.380 --> 00:01:02.620
First, Big Corp's public
website, of course,

00:01:02.620 --> 00:01:04.110
needs to be
accessible by anybody

00:01:04.110 --> 00:01:06.960
out there on the
internet, anonymous users.

00:01:06.960 --> 00:01:10.500
Second, their support
area of their website

00:01:10.500 --> 00:01:12.970
needs to be accessible
by anybody as well,

00:01:12.970 --> 00:01:14.820
but we have to know who it is.

00:01:14.820 --> 00:01:16.560
Users have to log
in so they only

00:01:16.560 --> 00:01:19.560
see their own support tickets.

00:01:19.560 --> 00:01:23.820
Third, employees at the
company, and contractors,

00:01:23.820 --> 00:01:25.830
and occasional
other people who are

00:01:25.830 --> 00:01:27.870
on the inside of
the company, will

00:01:27.870 --> 00:01:31.800
need to be able to see the
intranet and their internal web

00:01:31.800 --> 00:01:33.290
applications.

00:01:33.290 --> 00:01:37.740
And fourth, some employees
in certain teams,

00:01:37.740 --> 00:01:39.570
like support team,
they'll really

00:01:39.570 --> 00:01:43.590
get to see customer PII,
Personally Identifiable Data.

00:01:43.590 --> 00:01:47.520
But only if they are
on secured machines.

00:01:47.520 --> 00:01:50.400
So Charlie, can
Google Cloud Platform

00:01:50.400 --> 00:01:53.430
help Marissa with these
four access levels?

00:01:53.430 --> 00:01:54.270
CHARLIE: Yes it can.

00:01:54.270 --> 00:01:56.490
I've been in Marissa's
situation in a previous job

00:01:56.490 --> 00:01:58.440
and also created
new website services

00:01:58.440 --> 00:02:01.620
that require user authentication
and access control.

00:02:01.620 --> 00:02:04.560
So I know this can
be a very challenging

00:02:04.560 --> 00:02:08.759
technological management
and configuration issue.

00:02:08.759 --> 00:02:12.360
The Google Cloud platform has a
tool that can help enormously.

00:02:12.360 --> 00:02:14.930
It's called
Identity-Aware Proxy.

00:02:14.930 --> 00:02:17.820
It's a great fit for
Marissa and her developers,

00:02:17.820 --> 00:02:20.250
and it's a service
that controls access

00:02:20.250 --> 00:02:21.990
to those websites for them.

00:02:21.990 --> 00:02:25.380
The nice thing is it can do
this without changing those web

00:02:25.380 --> 00:02:28.910
servers in any way.

00:02:28.910 --> 00:02:30.080
MARTIN: That sounds great.

00:02:30.080 --> 00:02:31.790
Just the ticket for Marissa.

00:02:31.790 --> 00:02:35.060
How would this actually
work in practice?

00:02:35.060 --> 00:02:37.070
CHARLIE: Well, let's take
a look at an example.

00:02:37.070 --> 00:02:39.500
Here, we've got an
internet-facing website

00:02:39.500 --> 00:02:41.610
that needs to control access.

00:02:41.610 --> 00:02:45.020
And it's got three potential
users on this picture.

00:02:45.020 --> 00:02:47.810
The general public, somebody
that doesn't authenticate

00:02:47.810 --> 00:02:50.780
themselves at all, a
real authorized user,

00:02:50.780 --> 00:02:52.700
and then somebody
that can authenticate,

00:02:52.700 --> 00:02:54.410
they've got credentials,
but they're not

00:02:54.410 --> 00:02:56.840
one of the people the
site wants to talk to.

00:02:56.840 --> 00:02:59.703
So right now, all of their
requests hit this server.

00:02:59.703 --> 00:03:01.370
And it's the web
server's responsibility

00:03:01.370 --> 00:03:05.960
to decide which ones to respond
to and which ones to ignore.

00:03:05.960 --> 00:03:08.450
Identity-Aware
Proxy is a service

00:03:08.450 --> 00:03:14.870
that sits right here, between
the server and the internet.

00:03:14.870 --> 00:03:17.790
All the requests coming from
the internet to the web server

00:03:17.790 --> 00:03:20.180
now go through
Identity-Aware Proxy.

00:03:20.180 --> 00:03:23.630
Identity-Aware Proxy
prompts users to log in.

00:03:23.630 --> 00:03:25.940
And after they log in,
it uses their identity

00:03:25.940 --> 00:03:29.970
and Marissa's configuration
to decide who to let through.

00:03:29.970 --> 00:03:33.330
And only requests
coming specifically

00:03:33.330 --> 00:03:36.450
from the authorized
users ever actually

00:03:36.450 --> 00:03:39.420
hit the web server at all.

00:03:39.420 --> 00:03:43.020
Now, in order to do this,
the users do have to log in.

00:03:43.020 --> 00:03:46.400
And they use Google's
Identity Service,

00:03:46.400 --> 00:03:50.330
which handles Gmail
and G Suite, basically

00:03:50.330 --> 00:03:53.670
all of those different
possible addresses.

00:03:53.670 --> 00:03:54.530
MARTIN: OK.

00:03:54.530 --> 00:03:56.090
That sounds pretty good.

00:03:56.090 --> 00:04:00.220
But how about if Big Corp
isn't using a Google identity?

00:04:00.220 --> 00:04:02.850
I know, for example, my
last employer before Google,

00:04:02.850 --> 00:04:06.040
they use Active
Directory instead.

00:04:06.040 --> 00:04:08.080
CHARLIE: That's
certainly pretty common.

00:04:08.080 --> 00:04:12.250
And Google solves
that with IAP as well.

00:04:12.250 --> 00:04:15.250
Marissa's corp can synchronize
their Active Directory

00:04:15.250 --> 00:04:17.269
with the Google
Identity Service,

00:04:17.269 --> 00:04:19.269
and then all the users
in their Active Directory

00:04:19.269 --> 00:04:23.850
can be verified by Google
and used to configure IAP.

00:04:23.850 --> 00:04:25.940
Once IAP knows who
each user is, it

00:04:25.940 --> 00:04:27.710
can simply block the
users that should not

00:04:27.710 --> 00:04:29.390
come through the website.

00:04:29.390 --> 00:04:31.023
They can't see it at all.

00:04:31.023 --> 00:04:32.690
And the web server
doesn't need to worry

00:04:32.690 --> 00:04:36.320
about is that somebody we should
respond to or not, because it

00:04:36.320 --> 00:04:39.200
never even gets a request
in the first place.

00:04:39.200 --> 00:04:40.310
MARTIN: Nice.

00:04:40.310 --> 00:04:43.130
Yeah, anybody who's had a web
server on the public internet

00:04:43.130 --> 00:04:45.680
knows that you get a
lot of weird traffic

00:04:45.680 --> 00:04:47.780
that you probably
don't want to see.

00:04:47.780 --> 00:04:49.240
CHARLIE: Absolutely

00:04:49.240 --> 00:04:50.840
MARTIN: So, Charlie,
this looks good,

00:04:50.840 --> 00:04:54.170
and we can keep the
unauthorized users out.

00:04:54.170 --> 00:04:56.960
But what if that web server
over there on the left

00:04:56.960 --> 00:05:00.940
actually needs to
know who is accessing?

00:05:00.940 --> 00:05:03.310
CHARLIE: Well, this picture
shows IAP controlling

00:05:03.310 --> 00:05:06.010
who can come through and have
the request come to the web

00:05:06.010 --> 00:05:06.840
server.

00:05:06.840 --> 00:05:10.630
But IAP has another trick up
its sleeve that I really love.

00:05:10.630 --> 00:05:14.480
When it sends a request
through it, it adds a header.

00:05:14.480 --> 00:05:15.760
In fact, it has three headers.

00:05:15.760 --> 00:05:17.770
And those headers
include the identity

00:05:17.770 --> 00:05:20.410
of the user that
authenticated IAP.

00:05:20.410 --> 00:05:23.530
So now if the web server does
want to know who that user is,

00:05:23.530 --> 00:05:25.413
it could just check a header.

00:05:25.413 --> 00:05:26.830
It's hard to have
something easier

00:05:26.830 --> 00:05:30.430
than that in any kind of
a web server framework.

00:05:30.430 --> 00:05:31.910
So it could find
the email address.

00:05:31.910 --> 00:05:33.982
It can find a persistent ID.

00:05:33.982 --> 00:05:35.440
There's a lot of
information that's

00:05:35.440 --> 00:05:38.690
available in those headers.

00:05:38.690 --> 00:05:39.980
MARTIN: OK, cool.

00:05:39.980 --> 00:05:43.820
You mentioned different
frameworks and platforms.

00:05:43.820 --> 00:05:45.330
And, of course,
Marissa's company,

00:05:45.330 --> 00:05:48.350
they have lots of different
frameworks and platforms.

00:05:48.350 --> 00:05:51.860
Which of these
does IAP work with?

00:05:51.860 --> 00:05:53.480
CHARLIE: As you'd
expect, IAP can

00:05:53.480 --> 00:05:56.420
protect services running on a
lot of Google Cloud platform

00:05:56.420 --> 00:05:59.900
infrastructure, such as
App Engine, Compute Engine,

00:05:59.900 --> 00:06:02.900
and pretty much anything that
could be behind a Cloud Load

00:06:02.900 --> 00:06:04.840
Balancer.

00:06:04.840 --> 00:06:08.460
But it also can protect stuff
on the company's own premises

00:06:08.460 --> 00:06:10.800
using something
called IAP connector.

00:06:10.800 --> 00:06:13.530
That's going to require some
extra network configuration

00:06:13.530 --> 00:06:16.980
to route the requests from
IAP back on to the company's

00:06:16.980 --> 00:06:18.120
own premises.

00:06:18.120 --> 00:06:21.030
But once it's set up, then it's
configured for access, just

00:06:21.030 --> 00:06:23.760
like any of the other
things behind IAP.

00:06:23.760 --> 00:06:25.660
All this is straightforward

00:06:25.660 --> 00:06:26.160
MARTIN: OK.

00:06:26.160 --> 00:06:29.430
It looks pretty straightforward
here in slide four.

00:06:29.430 --> 00:06:31.950
How would Marissa actually
go in and set this up,

00:06:31.950 --> 00:06:34.728
in real, practical terms?

00:06:34.728 --> 00:06:36.270
CHARLIE: All right,
well, I've set up

00:06:36.270 --> 00:06:39.090
a demo project on
Google Cloud platform

00:06:39.090 --> 00:06:41.190
with five sample websites.

00:06:41.190 --> 00:06:44.310
Let's take a look at
that on the console.

00:06:44.310 --> 00:06:48.090
Here we see the console
control page for App Engine,

00:06:48.090 --> 00:06:51.630
and there are five websites
set up here for App Engine.

00:06:51.630 --> 00:06:55.860
One of them is the public one
at just plain bigcorpltd.com.

00:06:55.860 --> 00:06:59.400
There's one for customers
to send in support requests,

00:06:59.400 --> 00:07:01.650
one for the customer support
staff to respond to them,

00:07:01.650 --> 00:07:03.030
and a couple of
internal websites

00:07:03.030 --> 00:07:04.165
for different purposes.

00:07:04.165 --> 00:07:05.540
One that's open
to all employees,

00:07:05.540 --> 00:07:07.590
one that's more restricted.

00:07:07.590 --> 00:07:10.635
IAP can configure all
of these situations.

00:07:10.635 --> 00:07:12.840
Now right now, all
five of these websites

00:07:12.840 --> 00:07:15.900
are wide open because
we haven't enabled IAP,

00:07:15.900 --> 00:07:18.950
and we're going to fix that.

00:07:18.950 --> 00:07:19.550
MARTIN: Right.

00:07:19.550 --> 00:07:21.210
Yeah, that doesn't
seem very safe.

00:07:21.210 --> 00:07:23.810
Is there some way
Marissa can not

00:07:23.810 --> 00:07:26.870
expose the company internal
stuff, not even for a minute,

00:07:26.870 --> 00:07:29.100
not even for a second?

00:07:29.100 --> 00:07:31.270
CHARLIE: Well, it
turns out that in order

00:07:31.270 --> 00:07:33.450
to turn on IAP to
protect something,

00:07:33.450 --> 00:07:35.610
something needs to
be there already.

00:07:35.610 --> 00:07:37.560
So what I would
do is I would just

00:07:37.560 --> 00:07:40.950
deploy a simple hello, world
website so I could enable IAP.

00:07:40.950 --> 00:07:42.510
And then when I put
more websites out

00:07:42.510 --> 00:07:45.240
in the same project, they
would be protected by default.

00:07:45.240 --> 00:07:47.520
And I could then configure
them to whatever way I

00:07:47.520 --> 00:07:52.240
wanted them to be so it could
all be a little bit different.

00:07:52.240 --> 00:07:54.713
OK, so let's get started.

00:07:54.713 --> 00:07:57.130
Here we have our App Engine
project with a lot of websites

00:07:57.130 --> 00:08:01.660
to protect, so we've got to turn
IAP, or Identity-Aware Proxy,

00:08:01.660 --> 00:08:04.570
on for it using
the card console.

00:08:04.570 --> 00:08:07.720
The Cloud Console menu
has the different commands

00:08:07.720 --> 00:08:13.020
organized in groups, and IAP
is under the security group.

00:08:13.020 --> 00:08:13.895
Identity-Aware Proxy.

00:08:17.240 --> 00:08:21.350
So here we see all
the different HTTPS,

00:08:21.350 --> 00:08:25.580
that is web servers, that
are protectable by IAP.

00:08:25.580 --> 00:08:27.680
All those App Engine ones are.

00:08:27.680 --> 00:08:30.620
To protect them, all I got
to do is flip this toggle.

00:08:36.289 --> 00:08:36.789
That's it--

00:08:36.789 --> 00:08:37.020
MARTIN: That was easy.

00:08:37.020 --> 00:08:38.460
CHARLIE: --they're protected.

00:08:38.460 --> 00:08:39.014
Now, if this were--

00:08:39.014 --> 00:08:39.059
MARTIN: Yeah.

00:08:39.059 --> 00:08:41.684
CHARLIE: --the first time I ever
turned on IAP in this project,

00:08:41.684 --> 00:08:44.370
I would be prompted to
fill in a form for what

00:08:44.370 --> 00:08:47.070
we call the consent screen.

00:08:47.070 --> 00:08:49.050
And that basically
is describing who's

00:08:49.050 --> 00:08:50.402
asking the user to log in.

00:08:50.402 --> 00:08:52.110
So when Google puts
the launch screen up,

00:08:52.110 --> 00:08:55.350
it can say this is for
bigcorpltd.com, that's

00:08:55.350 --> 00:08:57.390
who you're logging in to.

00:08:57.390 --> 00:08:58.560
MARTIN: All right.

00:08:58.560 --> 00:09:02.280
So now this is set up here.

00:09:02.280 --> 00:09:06.588
And what happens if you
hit the public website now?

00:09:06.588 --> 00:09:08.130
CHARLIE: Well, it
takes a few minutes

00:09:08.130 --> 00:09:10.020
for changes to IAP to
propagate, because it's

00:09:10.020 --> 00:09:12.180
a worldwide service.

00:09:12.180 --> 00:09:15.850
But once that's done, we'll
open a fresh incognito browser,

00:09:15.850 --> 00:09:19.680
so there's no logged-in
user already cached,

00:09:19.680 --> 00:09:22.760
and try to connect to
the corporate website.

00:09:26.610 --> 00:09:29.390
And I'm immediately
asked to sign in.

00:09:29.390 --> 00:09:32.865
Well, I can sign in with
my bigcorpltd.com account.

00:09:35.660 --> 00:09:37.410
MARTIN: Oh, you work
at Big Corp, Charlie.

00:09:37.410 --> 00:09:39.010
I didn't know.

00:09:39.010 --> 00:09:40.580
CHARLIE: Just temporarily.

00:09:40.580 --> 00:09:41.080
And--

00:09:41.080 --> 00:09:41.913
MARTIN: Temporarily.

00:09:41.913 --> 00:09:44.595
CHARLIE: --now I see
that IAP is blocking me.

00:09:44.595 --> 00:09:47.120
MARTIN: Uh oh.

00:09:47.120 --> 00:09:50.240
But that is a little
weird, because I did not

00:09:50.240 --> 00:09:54.410
see you turn off access
for anybody, Charlie.

00:09:54.410 --> 00:09:58.430
CHARLIE: I did not tell
IAP to deny anybody access.

00:09:58.430 --> 00:10:00.200
But I turned on IAP,
and I didn't tell

00:10:00.200 --> 00:10:02.060
it to let anybody in, either.

00:10:02.060 --> 00:10:04.960
And that's the way it works IAP
doesn't say who to keep out,

00:10:04.960 --> 00:10:06.500
it says who to let in.

00:10:06.500 --> 00:10:08.870
So we're going to need to go
in to tell IAP to let users

00:10:08.870 --> 00:10:12.360
in to this public site.

00:10:12.360 --> 00:10:15.940
Let's go ahead and
configure the public site.

00:10:15.940 --> 00:10:18.520
Remember the use cases
we were looking at.

00:10:18.520 --> 00:10:20.230
The first one is
everybody in the world

00:10:20.230 --> 00:10:21.397
should be able to get to it.

00:10:21.397 --> 00:10:25.620
This is just a regular
public commercial website.

00:10:25.620 --> 00:10:28.270
MARTIN: Yeah so this
seems a little odd,

00:10:28.270 --> 00:10:31.250
I think, for IAP,
because it worked,

00:10:31.250 --> 00:10:33.910
it was public for
everybody a minute ago.

00:10:33.910 --> 00:10:36.820
We now turned it off with IAP.

00:10:36.820 --> 00:10:39.958
Do we even need IAP
for this use case?

00:10:39.958 --> 00:10:40.750
CHARLIE: Well, yes.

00:10:40.750 --> 00:10:43.508
This is kind of an odd use
case because we had a site that

00:10:43.508 --> 00:10:44.800
was already open to the public.

00:10:44.800 --> 00:10:46.890
We turned on IAP,
nobody's allowed in.

00:10:46.890 --> 00:10:49.880
And now we're going to configure
IAP to let everybody in again.

00:10:49.880 --> 00:10:53.960
The reason for this is that IAP
is global across this project.

00:10:53.960 --> 00:10:56.590
So the public website is in
the same Google Cloud project

00:10:56.590 --> 00:10:59.590
as the other ones, so
when I turn on IAP,

00:10:59.590 --> 00:11:01.180
it affects it as well.

00:11:01.180 --> 00:11:03.640
Luckily, I can tell IAP,
pretend you're not there.

00:11:03.640 --> 00:11:04.948
Let the public through.

00:11:04.948 --> 00:11:06.490
Let's go ahead and
do that right now.

00:11:06.490 --> 00:11:08.890
Take a look at how
we configure it.

00:11:08.890 --> 00:11:12.620
Here we see the public website,
which is the default one.

00:11:12.620 --> 00:11:14.890
When we select it,
we have an info panel

00:11:14.890 --> 00:11:19.300
that shows who's got
various access to the site.

00:11:19.300 --> 00:11:21.490
And what we want to do is
add a member to the site,

00:11:21.490 --> 00:11:23.770
this is how we let
somebody through,

00:11:23.770 --> 00:11:26.140
and we just need to put,
say, their email addresses.

00:11:26.140 --> 00:11:29.470
So we can put everybody's email
address in the world here.

00:11:29.470 --> 00:11:31.410
But that's not really
what we want to do.

00:11:31.410 --> 00:11:32.702
MARTIN: That's a lot of typing.

00:11:32.702 --> 00:11:35.505
CHARLIE: So there's
actually a special case

00:11:35.505 --> 00:11:37.630
that I need to type.

00:11:37.630 --> 00:11:40.810
All users is a special IAP user.

00:11:40.810 --> 00:11:45.640
To give anybody access, I need
to give them an IAP secure web

00:11:45.640 --> 00:11:47.980
app user.

00:11:47.980 --> 00:11:50.560
Save that.

00:11:50.560 --> 00:11:53.080
Yes, I want to
allow public access.

00:11:53.080 --> 00:11:55.390
And remember, it'll
take a couple minutes.

00:11:55.390 --> 00:11:58.462
But now, after
that's propagated,

00:11:58.462 --> 00:11:59.920
when somebody goes
to that website,

00:11:59.920 --> 00:12:01.270
they won't even see IAP at all.

00:12:01.270 --> 00:12:03.050
They won't be
prompted to log in,

00:12:03.050 --> 00:12:05.540
they'll just be sent
directly to the site.

00:12:05.540 --> 00:12:06.040
OK.

00:12:06.040 --> 00:12:07.460
We've waited a
couple of minutes,

00:12:07.460 --> 00:12:09.580
so the changes
should be propagated.

00:12:09.580 --> 00:12:14.380
Let's open a fresh
incognito web browser.

00:12:14.380 --> 00:12:18.304
So nobody's been logged into
anything through it yet.

00:12:18.304 --> 00:12:20.220
MARTIN: Mm-hmm.

00:12:20.220 --> 00:12:23.490
CHARLIE: And it'll take
a second to go through.

00:12:23.490 --> 00:12:24.930
Didn't prompt me to log in.

00:12:24.930 --> 00:12:27.560
Anybody in the world
was allowed to connect.

00:12:27.560 --> 00:12:29.835
Just as if we didn't
have IAP at all.

00:12:29.835 --> 00:12:30.960
There are probably easier--

00:12:30.960 --> 00:12:31.090
MARTIN: All right.

00:12:31.090 --> 00:12:32.840
CHARLIE: --ways to write
that than turning on IAP

00:12:32.840 --> 00:12:34.050
and then configuring it.

00:12:34.050 --> 00:12:36.050
But we're going to see
more valuable uses of IAP

00:12:36.050 --> 00:12:37.890
in a few minutes,

00:12:37.890 --> 00:12:43.410
MARTIN: I see that Big Corp
like minimalist web design.

00:12:43.410 --> 00:12:46.450
CHARLIE: It was very
minimalist web design, yes.

00:12:46.450 --> 00:12:48.200
MARTIN: Yes.

00:12:48.200 --> 00:12:49.260
All right, so, cool.

00:12:49.260 --> 00:12:51.410
That was public access.

00:12:51.410 --> 00:12:55.240
Perhaps the simplest use case,
and maybe the least interesting

00:12:55.240 --> 00:12:55.740
one.

00:12:55.740 --> 00:12:57.470
Let's move on to the second one.

00:12:57.470 --> 00:12:59.520
I think this is a
lot more interesting.

00:12:59.520 --> 00:13:03.710
So users-- anybody can log in,
but they must be authenticated.

00:13:03.710 --> 00:13:06.143
How does IAP do this?

00:13:06.143 --> 00:13:08.060
CHARLIE: From the IP
management point of view,

00:13:08.060 --> 00:13:09.800
this is almost identical.

00:13:09.800 --> 00:13:11.995
We want IAP to let
everybody through.

00:13:11.995 --> 00:13:14.120
However, we're saying,
before you let them through,

00:13:14.120 --> 00:13:16.910
authenticate them, and pass
through headers telling us

00:13:16.910 --> 00:13:19.020
who you authenticated them as.

00:13:19.020 --> 00:13:21.590
So if we go back to the
console, instead of default,

00:13:21.590 --> 00:13:24.760
we go to the support site
the general public will use.

00:13:24.760 --> 00:13:26.400
And again, we add a member.

00:13:26.400 --> 00:13:30.180
And the only difference here
is, instead of adding all users,

00:13:30.180 --> 00:13:34.530
you add the special use case,
all authenticated users.

00:13:34.530 --> 00:13:40.160
Again, we grant them the role
of an IAP-secured web app user.

00:13:40.160 --> 00:13:40.660
Save it.

00:13:40.660 --> 00:13:42.952
And warns that we're making
it available to the public,

00:13:42.952 --> 00:13:44.950
but only authenticated
members of the public.

00:13:48.520 --> 00:13:52.750
And after it propagates
and we connect to it,

00:13:52.750 --> 00:13:55.990
we'll see that the
site knows who we are.

00:13:55.990 --> 00:13:56.590
OK.

00:13:56.590 --> 00:14:00.610
So let's go in a fresh
incognito browser,

00:14:00.610 --> 00:14:04.450
and go to that customer support
site, support.bigcorpltd.com.

00:14:09.590 --> 00:14:11.340
And we told this to
let everybody through,

00:14:11.340 --> 00:14:14.543
but we said authenticate them
before you let them through.

00:14:14.543 --> 00:14:16.460
I'm going to use the
same dummy email account,

00:14:16.460 --> 00:14:18.335
but it really doesn't
matter which one I use.

00:14:18.335 --> 00:14:21.939
It could be anybody that
Google can authenticate.

00:14:29.280 --> 00:14:30.193
Oops.

00:14:30.193 --> 00:14:32.610
And it can only authenticate
me if I type the password in,

00:14:32.610 --> 00:14:33.110
right?

00:14:35.520 --> 00:14:38.550
Now I'll pass you
through to the account.

00:14:38.550 --> 00:14:41.520
Take a second for the website
to spin up to respond.

00:14:41.520 --> 00:14:45.642
And notice that it says
welcome, engelke@bigcorpltd.com.

00:14:45.642 --> 00:14:47.100
The website knows
my email address.

00:14:47.100 --> 00:14:47.740
MARTIN: That's your email.

00:14:47.740 --> 00:14:49.050
CHARLIE: I didn't
have to do anything.

00:14:49.050 --> 00:14:49.600
MARTIN: Yeah.

00:14:49.600 --> 00:14:51.600
CHARLIE: I didn't have
to log in to the website.

00:14:51.600 --> 00:14:55.530
IAP told the website who it was.

00:14:55.530 --> 00:14:57.840
MARTIN: So how does IAP do that?

00:14:57.840 --> 00:14:59.777
How does this work?

00:14:59.777 --> 00:15:01.360
CHARLIE: Well, the
way it knows that--

00:15:01.360 --> 00:15:05.500
let's go look at the
picture we had earlier.

00:15:05.500 --> 00:15:09.160
Of all your tests coming in and
being intercepted and adding

00:15:09.160 --> 00:15:11.800
headers, these are the
headers that it adds.

00:15:11.800 --> 00:15:15.930
Three headers, one called
X-Goog-Authenticated-User-Email,

00:15:15.930 --> 00:15:19.260
then
X-Goog-Authenticated-User-Id,

00:15:19.260 --> 00:15:23.580
and X-Goog-Iap-Jwt-Assertion.

00:15:23.580 --> 00:15:25.770
Here are some examples
of the values.

00:15:25.770 --> 00:15:28.760
The authenticated user email
one is my email address

00:15:28.760 --> 00:15:31.390
that IAP verifies,
it stands behind it

00:15:31.390 --> 00:15:32.440
saying I really checked.

00:15:32.440 --> 00:15:35.010
That's who this really is.

00:15:35.010 --> 00:15:39.530
The user ID is kind of an opaque
identifier that Google uses,

00:15:39.530 --> 00:15:41.290
so that every time
I go to the website,

00:15:41.290 --> 00:15:43.960
I'll have the same
identifier, but the website

00:15:43.960 --> 00:15:46.450
won't necessarily
know my email address.

00:15:46.450 --> 00:15:49.150
That way it could store
that if it wanted to.

00:15:49.150 --> 00:15:51.850
Only give me my response
back, but without storing

00:15:51.850 --> 00:15:54.820
something potentially sensitive,
like an email address.

00:15:54.820 --> 00:15:58.070
The final one's a little
bit more complicated.

00:15:58.070 --> 00:15:59.980
It's the JWT header.

00:15:59.980 --> 00:16:02.578
MARTIN: What is the JWT header?

00:16:02.578 --> 00:16:03.870
CHARLIE: Well, that's showing--

00:16:03.870 --> 00:16:04.100
MARTIN: I see base64.

00:16:04.100 --> 00:16:06.880
CHARLIE: --it all because
this goes on and on and on.

00:16:06.880 --> 00:16:10.230
It's a very long basic
ensure encoded string

00:16:10.230 --> 00:16:13.120
that represents a
digitally-signed record that

00:16:13.120 --> 00:16:15.790
has a user ID and email in it,
which from the other headers

00:16:15.790 --> 00:16:17.350
anyway.

00:16:17.350 --> 00:16:20.020
But the fact is, depending on
how the company servers are

00:16:20.020 --> 00:16:23.390
configured, particularly if
you're on your own data center,

00:16:23.390 --> 00:16:26.500
you may have the path from the
internet to your website server

00:16:26.500 --> 00:16:28.160
that has to go through IAP.

00:16:28.160 --> 00:16:30.160
And you may have other
paths from other machines

00:16:30.160 --> 00:16:34.590
inside your data center network
that can send requests to IAP.

00:16:34.590 --> 00:16:37.360
And if you're concerned
that those other machines

00:16:37.360 --> 00:16:39.820
in your data center
could be compromised,

00:16:39.820 --> 00:16:42.235
they could send fake
requests to that web server,

00:16:42.235 --> 00:16:44.860
and they could add a header that
says this is by email address,

00:16:44.860 --> 00:16:47.500
even though it really isn't.

00:16:47.500 --> 00:16:50.645
The digitally-signed
JWT can't be spoofed,

00:16:50.645 --> 00:16:52.270
because it's digitally
signed by Google

00:16:52.270 --> 00:16:54.230
with Google's private key.

00:16:54.230 --> 00:16:58.525
So the website can simply verify
it and decode it, and make sure

00:16:58.525 --> 00:16:59.650
that it didn't get spoofed.

00:17:02.950 --> 00:17:05.480
So in the description
below this video,

00:17:05.480 --> 00:17:08.920
we've got a link that points
to the documentation on things

00:17:08.920 --> 00:17:11.480
like JWT in general.

00:17:11.480 --> 00:17:13.420
And there's a
codelab specifically

00:17:13.420 --> 00:17:17.800
on how to decode that for
the Flask Python program

00:17:17.800 --> 00:17:20.849
that's being protected by IPSec.

00:17:20.849 --> 00:17:21.780
MARTIN: OK.

00:17:21.780 --> 00:17:24.839
So what I'm hearing is,
for many developers,

00:17:24.839 --> 00:17:28.440
it'll be enough to just look
at the email or user ID header.

00:17:28.440 --> 00:17:33.300
But if you want additional
security within your project

00:17:33.300 --> 00:17:38.150
or data center, then you may
want to verify the JWT header.

00:17:38.150 --> 00:17:41.430
CHARLIE: Yes, the more
control of networking options

00:17:41.430 --> 00:17:45.690
that are in use, the more paths
there might be to the server

00:17:45.690 --> 00:17:48.510
on the back end that don't
go through the front door.

00:17:48.510 --> 00:17:50.140
And then you'd be
concerned about it.

00:17:50.140 --> 00:17:52.140
But in a case like
this with App Engine,

00:17:52.140 --> 00:17:54.358
there aren't any such
paths, for our example.

00:17:54.358 --> 00:17:55.650
So we can do it the simple way.

00:17:55.650 --> 00:17:57.030
MARTIN: Oh, OK.

00:17:57.030 --> 00:17:58.170
Oh, excellent.

00:17:58.170 --> 00:17:58.890
All right, cool.

00:17:58.890 --> 00:18:00.990
So we talked about the
first two use cases.

00:18:00.990 --> 00:18:03.050
They're about public
access to the website.

00:18:03.050 --> 00:18:05.300
Now what about the
third use case?

00:18:05.300 --> 00:18:07.800
So remember, this was the
one where only Big Corp

00:18:07.800 --> 00:18:12.360
employees should be able to
hit their internal web apps.

00:18:12.360 --> 00:18:14.430
CHARLIE: Yeah, that
everybody needs to do.

00:18:14.430 --> 00:18:18.000
And when people first started
doing these internet sites,

00:18:18.000 --> 00:18:20.770
they would just take an
unprotected web server,

00:18:20.770 --> 00:18:22.920
but put it on the
internal company network.

00:18:22.920 --> 00:18:25.170
So anybody else that's on
the internal company network

00:18:25.170 --> 00:18:27.960
can get to it, people who
aren't can't get to it.

00:18:27.960 --> 00:18:28.540
Great.

00:18:28.540 --> 00:18:29.040
But it--

00:18:29.040 --> 00:18:29.370
MARTIN: Yeah.

00:18:29.370 --> 00:18:30.180
CHARLIE: --opened a
lot of other problems.

00:18:30.180 --> 00:18:31.472
MARTIN: What's wrong with that?

00:18:31.472 --> 00:18:34.780
CHARLIE: That website
trusted everybody

00:18:34.780 --> 00:18:37.450
on the internal company network,
every device that was there,

00:18:37.450 --> 00:18:39.130
which might not be a good idea.

00:18:39.130 --> 00:18:39.825
And an even bigger problem--

00:18:39.825 --> 00:18:39.960
MARTIN: Oh.

00:18:39.960 --> 00:18:40.890
CHARLIE: --that
occurred over time

00:18:40.890 --> 00:18:43.770
is employees are less and less
likely to be in the main office

00:18:43.770 --> 00:18:45.410
and connected to that
internal network.

00:18:45.410 --> 00:18:46.990
They'd be on the road.

00:18:46.990 --> 00:18:48.373
So the solution
to that was let's

00:18:48.373 --> 00:18:50.040
set up a virtual
private network so they

00:18:50.040 --> 00:18:52.740
can connect to the internal
network through the internet

00:18:52.740 --> 00:18:55.108
as if they were on
the internal network.

00:18:55.108 --> 00:18:57.150
That gave them the access,
but it opened up again

00:18:57.150 --> 00:18:58.860
a whole new set of problems.

00:18:58.860 --> 00:19:01.050
It was hard to
configure and support,

00:19:01.050 --> 00:19:02.850
and it meant that this
outside machine now

00:19:02.850 --> 00:19:06.810
had access to everything, not
just the employee's website.

00:19:06.810 --> 00:19:10.280
IAP gives us another solution.

00:19:10.280 --> 00:19:16.750
So remember the picture of
how IAP protected access.

00:19:16.750 --> 00:19:19.060
All other requests
had to go through it

00:19:19.060 --> 00:19:20.440
and it only let
a request through

00:19:20.440 --> 00:19:22.420
if it was one of the
users you wanted.

00:19:22.420 --> 00:19:25.777
So Marissa can simply
configure IAP and say, only let

00:19:25.777 --> 00:19:27.610
through people who
really are employees, who

00:19:27.610 --> 00:19:30.020
can prove they're employees.

00:19:30.020 --> 00:19:32.175
So the web requests
that come through IAP

00:19:32.175 --> 00:19:33.550
will just be this
kind of request

00:19:33.550 --> 00:19:36.220
that might have come in your
internal network for employees.

00:19:36.220 --> 00:19:39.100
You're protected from all
the rest of the internet.

00:19:39.100 --> 00:19:42.010
And the end user
doesn't need to have

00:19:42.010 --> 00:19:44.018
a VPN client or anything else.

00:19:44.018 --> 00:19:45.810
They can just connect
with any web browser.

00:19:45.810 --> 00:19:49.730
So long as they can
authenticate themselves.

00:19:49.730 --> 00:19:53.150
Now, configuring this
is very similar to what

00:19:53.150 --> 00:19:55.070
we've seen before.

00:19:55.070 --> 00:19:57.430
So let's go back
to that console.

00:19:57.430 --> 00:20:00.840
And let's go to the
employee website.

00:20:00.840 --> 00:20:04.860
And we need to add a member that
represents all the employees.

00:20:04.860 --> 00:20:10.150
Well, Marissa's company
synchronizes their Active

00:20:10.150 --> 00:20:12.790
Directory for all email
addresses at bigcorpltd.com

00:20:12.790 --> 00:20:14.930
to Google.

00:20:14.930 --> 00:20:20.125
So all they need to do
is say bigcorpltd.com,

00:20:20.125 --> 00:20:22.810
and every one of those email
addresses is now allowed

00:20:22.810 --> 00:20:25.660
and no others are.

00:20:25.660 --> 00:20:27.400
Typically, they may
have other people

00:20:27.400 --> 00:20:29.715
that want to treat as
employees for the intranet,

00:20:29.715 --> 00:20:31.840
but who they don't want to
give email addresses to.

00:20:31.840 --> 00:20:34.120
Maybe they're temps or
they're contractors.

00:20:34.120 --> 00:20:37.210
So they can set up a group
with all those email addresses,

00:20:37.210 --> 00:20:38.808
and they can add
the group as well.

00:20:38.808 --> 00:20:40.850
They don't have to enter
each individual address.

00:20:44.190 --> 00:20:47.200
And then HR can manage that
group as new temps come in

00:20:47.200 --> 00:20:49.640
and new contractors go out.

00:20:49.640 --> 00:20:52.300
While enabling it is the same
thing we've seen twice before.

00:20:52.300 --> 00:20:56.180
Give them the IAP secured
web app user role.

00:21:00.660 --> 00:21:01.950
And now, in a few minutes--

00:21:01.950 --> 00:21:03.450
we're not going to
wait and show it,

00:21:03.450 --> 00:21:05.658
'cause it's the same kind
of thing we've seen before.

00:21:05.658 --> 00:21:08.200
But anybody that tries
to go this employee site

00:21:08.200 --> 00:21:09.820
will be intercepted by IAP.

00:21:09.820 --> 00:21:11.890
And only if they're
an employee or one

00:21:11.890 --> 00:21:14.590
of these people in that group
of temps and contractors will

00:21:14.590 --> 00:21:15.910
they get through.

00:21:15.910 --> 00:21:18.670
Let's look at the
customer support site

00:21:18.670 --> 00:21:20.860
that the customer
support staff uses.

00:21:20.860 --> 00:21:22.360
They're going to
log in, and they're

00:21:22.360 --> 00:21:23.620
going to see the
requests from users,

00:21:23.620 --> 00:21:26.120
and they're going to see those
users' email addresses, which

00:21:26.120 --> 00:21:28.060
we don't want the
public in any case,

00:21:28.060 --> 00:21:29.905
ever, to be able to get to.

00:21:29.905 --> 00:21:32.530
So we want to make sure that not
only are the people connecting

00:21:32.530 --> 00:21:34.390
a member of the
customer support staff,

00:21:34.390 --> 00:21:37.660
but if they're using, say,
a personal laptop, that they

00:21:37.660 --> 00:21:39.850
don't use something that
doesn't have a screen lock,

00:21:39.850 --> 00:21:43.343
and they leave it logged in,
they walk away for a minute,

00:21:43.343 --> 00:21:45.760
and somebody steals it, they're
still logged into the site

00:21:45.760 --> 00:21:47.140
and see this data.

00:21:47.140 --> 00:21:49.850
You want to make sure that
we can't have that happen.

00:21:49.850 --> 00:21:52.380
So Marissa's company said,
we want the support staff

00:21:52.380 --> 00:21:54.130
to be able to connect
when they're remote.

00:21:54.130 --> 00:21:55.932
That's very, very common.

00:21:55.932 --> 00:21:57.890
And we want to restrict
it to only those staff.

00:21:57.890 --> 00:21:59.585
We want to go further.

00:21:59.585 --> 00:22:01.210
We only want them to
connect if they're

00:22:01.210 --> 00:22:04.450
using a machine that has a
screen lock enabled and has

00:22:04.450 --> 00:22:05.920
the hard drive encrypted.

00:22:05.920 --> 00:22:07.480
So if the device
is lost it doesn't

00:22:07.480 --> 00:22:12.490
give the person who takes
it access to that site.

00:22:12.490 --> 00:22:13.320
MARTIN: Oh.

00:22:13.320 --> 00:22:14.940
Can IAP do that?

00:22:14.940 --> 00:22:17.670
Does it know when
machines have been stolen?

00:22:17.670 --> 00:22:19.380
CHARLIE: Yes it can.

00:22:19.380 --> 00:22:23.050
This is one last little
trick IAP has for the moment.

00:22:23.050 --> 00:22:26.010
Configuring it to do that looks
a lot like the other use cases

00:22:26.010 --> 00:22:28.630
with a little bit of
a twist at the end.

00:22:28.630 --> 00:22:32.010
Let's go ahead and take
a look and do that.

00:22:32.010 --> 00:22:37.760
So here we see the IAP page and
the customer support website.

00:22:37.760 --> 00:22:40.700
We're going to add members of
the customer support group.

00:22:49.100 --> 00:22:54.990
And we're going to give them
IAP-secured Web App User.

00:22:54.990 --> 00:22:57.320
But before we say go
ahead and save this,

00:22:57.320 --> 00:23:00.530
we're going to
add a restriction.

00:23:00.530 --> 00:23:02.480
Only let them do it
if they're connecting

00:23:02.480 --> 00:23:05.585
from a machine that meets
the needs of a safer client.

00:23:10.060 --> 00:23:13.090
Now, when somebody from
the customer support group

00:23:13.090 --> 00:23:16.180
logs in, even if they
properly authenticate,

00:23:16.180 --> 00:23:17.680
they won't be let
through if they're

00:23:17.680 --> 00:23:19.900
on a machine that doesn't
have a screen lock

00:23:19.900 --> 00:23:23.220
or doesn't have an
encrypted hard drive.

00:23:23.220 --> 00:23:26.900
Now, that's all configured
in Access Context Manager.

00:23:26.900 --> 00:23:28.430
Here's that safer client policy.

00:23:28.430 --> 00:23:31.850
You can see it says devices have
to have a device screen lock

00:23:31.850 --> 00:23:34.100
and they have to have
encryption turned on.

00:23:34.100 --> 00:23:35.980
Enable.

00:23:35.980 --> 00:23:38.340
Just for a quick look, there
are a lot of other things

00:23:38.340 --> 00:23:41.850
that can be put in as well
if they were appropriate.

00:23:41.850 --> 00:23:43.970
For example, they
can restrict access

00:23:43.970 --> 00:23:48.530
to specific IP network ranges.

00:23:48.530 --> 00:23:51.387
They can also restrict
access to certain regions.

00:23:51.387 --> 00:23:52.970
Perhaps they want
to only allow people

00:23:52.970 --> 00:23:55.640
to access it from
the US and Canada,

00:23:55.640 --> 00:23:58.250
or only from European
Union countries.

00:23:58.250 --> 00:24:00.500
They can select those
countries, and only those people

00:24:00.500 --> 00:24:02.460
will come through.

00:24:02.460 --> 00:24:06.060
The device policy we are using
can be made more restrictive.

00:24:06.060 --> 00:24:08.160
The machine not only might
have to have a screen

00:24:08.160 --> 00:24:10.530
lock and encrypted
storage, but maybe it also

00:24:10.530 --> 00:24:12.810
has to be a machine that's
been specifically approved,

00:24:12.810 --> 00:24:16.040
or even a machine
that the company owns.

00:24:16.040 --> 00:24:19.130
And it can also restrict which
operating systems and operating

00:24:19.130 --> 00:24:22.950
system versions are allowed.

00:24:22.950 --> 00:24:26.940
I'm not going to add
any of those conditions,

00:24:26.940 --> 00:24:28.530
because this is
strong enough for what

00:24:28.530 --> 00:24:31.840
Marissa's company needs.

00:24:31.840 --> 00:24:37.660
Now, in order to
do that, IAP needs

00:24:37.660 --> 00:24:40.030
to know some stuff about
the end user's machine,

00:24:40.030 --> 00:24:43.060
like the screen lock and
the encryption level,

00:24:43.060 --> 00:24:45.130
that a browser normally
won't tell a web server,

00:24:45.130 --> 00:24:47.065
normally wouldn't tell IAP that.

00:24:47.065 --> 00:24:48.940
So the users are going
to have to have a work

00:24:48.940 --> 00:24:52.450
profile on their phone or Chrome
extension in their browser

00:24:52.450 --> 00:24:54.100
that tells them that.

00:24:54.100 --> 00:24:56.980
That's available for anybody,
if they are using G Suite

00:24:56.980 --> 00:24:59.140
and are connected to
a corporate profile,

00:24:59.140 --> 00:25:00.950
it'll do that automatically.

00:25:00.950 --> 00:25:02.560
And that basically
says, when you're

00:25:02.560 --> 00:25:05.000
connected to IAP
for our company,

00:25:05.000 --> 00:25:07.000
let it know this information
about your machine,

00:25:07.000 --> 00:25:10.940
so it can decide what
level of access to provide.

00:25:10.940 --> 00:25:12.840
And that's it.

00:25:12.840 --> 00:25:15.630
IAP can handle all
these four use cases,

00:25:15.630 --> 00:25:18.030
which are pretty universal.

00:25:18.030 --> 00:25:21.030
There's a lot of different ways
you configure and fine tune it.

00:25:21.030 --> 00:25:23.760
But the nice thing about
it is, Marissa can do that,

00:25:23.760 --> 00:25:25.620
and the app servers
that are out there

00:25:25.620 --> 00:25:27.450
don't have to change at all.

00:25:27.450 --> 00:25:29.560
MARTIN: Oh, nice.

00:25:29.560 --> 00:25:31.380
I think Big Corp
will eventually be

00:25:31.380 --> 00:25:33.960
able to delete a lot of
their authentication code

00:25:33.960 --> 00:25:37.358
they've sprinkled all
over the web apps.

00:25:37.358 --> 00:25:38.400
CHARLIE: I think so, too.

00:25:41.650 --> 00:25:43.690
MARTIN: Thank you for
showing us this, Charlie,

00:25:43.690 --> 00:25:46.600
and thank you all for tuning in.

00:25:46.600 --> 00:25:49.920
If you have questions about
how IAP works or any security

00:25:49.920 --> 00:25:53.970
questions on GCP and serverless,
please post them in comments

00:25:53.970 --> 00:25:54.720
below.

00:25:54.720 --> 00:25:57.540
Or if you have questions
about, or requests for,

00:25:57.540 --> 00:26:00.210
other videos we should create
around serverless, please

00:26:00.210 --> 00:26:01.720
let us know.

00:26:01.720 --> 00:26:02.920
Thank you for this time.

00:26:02.920 --> 00:26:04.848
See you next time.

